---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import ChuckDiagram from '../components/ChuckDiagram.astro';
import bottlingMachinery from '../assets/images/bottling-machinery.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Magnetic Capping Headsets: How They Work and When to Use Them"
    description="How magnetic capping headsets use hysteresis to set cap torque with no wearing parts. Specs, smooth vs. pulsating torque, spring-clutch and ROPP headsets, and which headset fits your line speed."
    canonical="https://cappingchucks.com/capping-headsets/"
    ogTitle="Magnetic Capping Headsets | CappingChucks.com"
    ogDescription="How magnetic capping headsets work, their specs, and when to pick magnetic, spring-clutch, or ROPP headsets."
    schemaType="TechArticle"
    breadcrumb="Magnetic Capping Headsets"
>
    <PageHero
        title="Magnetic Capping Headsets"
        crumb="Capping Headsets"
        answerLabel="What is a magnetic capping headset?"
        image={bottlingMachinery}
        alt="Glass bottles passing through capping machinery on a high-speed production line"
        caption="Capping station on a glass bottle line. Photo: Pexels"
    >
        <p>A magnetic capping headset is the full capping unit on a spindle. It uses magnets instead of rubbing parts to set how tight each cap goes on. It is the standard headset on rotary cappers running over 800 caps per minute.</p>
    </PageHero>

    <main>
        <section id="magnetic" class="section">
            <div class="wrap">
                <span class="kicker">How it works</span>
                <h2>How magnetic capping headsets set torque</h2>
                <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2 items-start">
                    <div>
                        <p>Inside the headset, two sets of magnetic plates face each other. One set turns with the motor. The other set turns the <a href="/">capping chuck</a>. The magnetic pull drags the second set along.</p>
                        <p>When the cap gets tight enough, the pull is no longer strong enough to keep turning it. The plates slip past each other without touching. This is called a <a href="/glossary/#hysteresis">hysteresis</a> coupling. The cap stops at the same torque every time.</p>
                        <p>Nothing rubs, so nothing wears out in the torque path. Torque stays steady over millions of caps. You still need to check it on a schedule, because the chuck, spindle, and caps still wear.</p>
                    </div>
                    <ChuckDiagram caption="On a magnetic headset, part 2 is a set of magnetic plates. They slip at the set torque without touching." />
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="specs" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <span class="kicker kicker-green">Specs</span>
                <h2>Magnetic headset specs</h2>
                <div class="specs prose-col">
                    <p class="specs-title">Typical numbers</p>
                    <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">How torque is set</span><span class="spec-value">DC current to the magnet coil</span></div>
                    <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Repeatability</span><span class="spec-value">&plusmn;0.1 N&middot;m on approved machines</span></div>
                    <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Torque spread (CV%)</span><span class="spec-value">Under 3% when well kept</span></div>
                    <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Parts that rub</span><span class="spec-value">None in the torque path</span></div>
                    <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Changing torque</span><span class="spec-value">PLC or analog signal, no tools</span></div>
                    <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Line speed</span><span class="spec-value">800 to 1,200+ caps per minute</span></div>
                </div>

                <h3 class="mt-10">Smooth torque vs. pulsing torque</h3>
                <div class="prose-col">
                    <p>Older magnetic headsets have magnets with opposite poles. As the plates turn, the pull jumps from pole to pole. Each jump makes a small torque spike. These spikes add wear to the machine and can make tamper bands seat unevenly on thin caps.</p>
                    <p>Newer headsets use a smooth design. The magnetic pull stays even all the way around. That means no spikes, less stress on the capper, and steadier <a href="/glossary/#slip-torque">slip torque</a> at every speed.</p>
                </div>

                <h3 class="mt-10">Will it fit my capper?</h3>
                <div class="prose-col">
                    <p>Most magnetic headsets are built to swap into machines from Zalkin, Krones, AROL, CSI, KHS, Federal, and Fogg. Aftermarket units copy the original spindle connection and size. Before you install one, check the spindle taper, how it locks in place, and the control signal. See the <a href="/oem-compatibility/">OEM compatibility guide</a>.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="headset-vs-chuck" class="section">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <span class="kicker kicker-blue">Words</span>
                <h2>Capping head vs. chuck</h2>
                <p>People use these words as if they mean the same thing. They don't. The chuck is only the part that grips the cap. The headset (or capping head) is the whole unit: the chuck, the torque clutch, the spindle adapter, and on ROPP lines, the thread rollers.</p>
                <p>If you order a "magnetic capping headset," you get the full unit with the magnetic clutch inside. If you order a "capping chuck," you usually get just the cap-gripping part that bolts onto a headset you already have.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="spring-clutch" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <span class="kicker">Other headsets</span>
                <h2>Spring-clutch headsets</h2>
                <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2 items-start">
                    <div>
                        <p>Spring-clutch headsets, also called friction headsets or capping clutches, use a stack of plates pressed together by a spring. When the cap gets tight enough, the plates slip. To set torque, you tighten or loosen the spring with a threaded collar or shims.</p>
                        <p>They are the normal pick for inline cappers and rotary lines under 600 caps per minute. They cost less than magnetic headsets. The downside is wear: the plates rub, so torque drifts over time. Replace plates on a schedule and recheck torque every time you do.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="specs">
                        <p class="specs-title">Spring-clutch specs</p>
                        <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">How torque is set</span><span class="spec-value">Spring pressure</span></div>
                        <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Plate materials</span><span class="spec-value">Sintered bronze, friction composite, ceramic</span></div>
                        <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Changing torque</span><span class="spec-value">Threaded collar or shims</span></div>
                        <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Line speed</span><span class="spec-value">200 to 600 caps per minute</span></div>
                        <div class="spec-row"><span class="spec-label">Upkeep</span><span class="spec-value">Replace clutch plates on a schedule</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="ropp" class="section">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <span class="kicker kicker-green">Aluminum caps</span>
                <h2>ROPP headsets</h2>
                <p>ROPP means roll-on pilfer-proof. These aluminum caps start with no threads. The headset makes the threads on the spot. A top pressure pad holds the cap down on the glass. Rollers press the soft aluminum into the bottle's threads. Other rollers tuck the bottom band under a ridge on the bottle neck.</p>
                <p>The roller angle, depth, and position must match the bottle's threads exactly. If they are off, you get weak threads, loose caps, or cracked glass. ROPP headsets are usually made for one machine brand.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="selection" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <span class="kicker kicker-blue">Pick one</span>
                <h2>Which headset fits your line speed?</h2>
                <div class="overflow-x-auto">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Caps per minute</th>
                                <th>Headset</th>
                                <th>Why</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Under 200</td>
                                <td>Spring-clutch or pneumatic</td>
                                <td>Too slow to justify magnetic cost. Pick pneumatic if you change cap types often.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>200 to 600</td>
                                <td>Spring-clutch</td>
                                <td>Handles this range well. Plate changes are easy to keep up with.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>600 to 800</td>
                                <td>Magnetic or spring-clutch</td>
                                <td>Either works. Go magnetic if torque spread must stay under 3%.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Over 800</td>
                                <td>Magnetic</td>
                                <td>No rubbing parts and no heat buildup at high speed. Standard on fast rotary lines.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="calibration" class="section">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <span class="kicker">Setup</span>
                <h2>How to calibrate a capper headset</h2>
                <p>Get the target torque from the cap maker. Set it with coil current on a magnetic headset, or spring pressure on a spring-clutch headset. Cap at least 30 bottles, test them with a calibrated torque tester, and check the spread (CV%). The full steps are in our <a href="/troubleshooting/#calibration">headset calibration guide</a>.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>Keep reading</h2>
                <ul class="related">
                    <li><a href="/chuck-types/">Capping chuck types</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/oem-compatibility/">OEM compatibility</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/glossary/">Glossary</a></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
